# 📘 Software Engineering Education Project

## Overview
This repository is a comprehensive collection of research notes and summaries on **software engineering fundamentals**.  
It covers the entire lifecycle of software development — from requirements engineering to design, agile practices, and quality assurance.  
The goal is to provide a structured, accessible reference for students, developers, and professionals.

---

## 📂 Contents
- [Software Processes](#software-processes) – lifecycle models, activities, coping with change, RUP.
- [Requirements Engineering](#requirements-engineering) – functional vs non-functional requirements, elicitation, validation, management.
- [Functional & Non-Functional Requirements](#functional--non-functional-requirements) – definitions, examples, challenges, and extended requirements.
- [Software Design](#software-design) – design levels, phases, architecture, components, and design documents.
- [Agile Development & XP](#agile-development--xp) – principles, practices, trade-offs with plan-driven methods, scaling agile.
- [Software Quality Assurance (SQA)](#software-quality-assurance-sqa) – standards, reviews, testing, risk management, benefits and challenges.

---

## Software Processes
- Fundamental activities: **Specification, Design & Implementation, Validation, Evolution**.
- Models: **Waterfall, Incremental Development, Reuse-Oriented Engineering, Spiral, RUP**.
- Coping with change: prototyping, incremental delivery.
- Key takeaway: change is inevitable, processes must anticipate and tolerate it.

[🔝 Back to Contents](#-contents)

---

## Requirements Engineering
- Defines **what a system should do** and its constraints.
- Types: **User requirements** (natural language, diagrams) vs **System requirements** (structured, detailed).
- Functional vs Non-functional requirements:
  - Functional → features/services (e.g., login, reporting).
  - Non-functional → qualities/constraints (e.g., performance, security).
- Processes: elicitation, specification, validation, management.
- Techniques: interviews, scenarios, use cases, ethnography.

[🔝 Back to Contents](#-contents)

---

## Functional & Non-Functional Requirements
- **Functional**: what the system does (authentication, data processing, transactions).
- **Non-functional**: how the system performs (speed, usability, reliability, scalability).
- Extended requirements: logging, monitoring, analytics, backup, disaster recovery.
- Examples:
  - Online banking → login, balance check, notifications.
  - Food delivery → ordering, payments, real-time tracking.
- Challenges: ambiguity, changing requirements, prioritization, measuring NFRs.

[🔝 Back to Contents](#-contents)

---

## Software Design
- Levels:
  - **Interface Design** → system/environment interactions.
  - **Architectural Design** → major components & relationships.
  - **Detailed Design** → algorithms, data structures, UI details.
- Phases: requirements → research → design → prototyping.
- Core elements: architecture, modules, components, interfaces, data.
- Software Design Document (SDD) sections: overview, architecture, data design, interfaces, components, UI, assumptions, glossary.
- Best practices: clarity, visuals, consistency, traceability, collaboration.

[🔝 Back to Contents](#-contents)

---

## Agile Development & XP
- Agile principles: customer involvement, incremental delivery, simplicity, embracing change.
- Extreme Programming (XP) practices:
  - Pair programming
  - Test-first development
  - Continuous integration
  - Refactoring
  - Collective ownership
- Agile vs Plan-driven trade-offs:
  - Agile → flexible, fast, evolving requirements.
  - Plan-driven → structured, documentation-heavy, suited for regulated/large systems.
- Scaling agile: adaptations for large projects and organizations.

[🔝 Back to Contents](#-contents)

---

## Software Quality Assurance (SQA)
- Works in parallel with development to ensure standards and processes are followed.
- Elements: standards, reviews, audits, testing, defect analysis, change management, education, security, safety, risk management.
- Focus areas: portability, usability, reusability, correctness, maintainability, error control.
- Benefits: higher quality, reliability, reduced maintenance costs.
- Challenges: cost, time, overhead, resource intensity, resistance to change.

